6:\"avatar\";s:121:\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a/ACg8ocJ7QJxogdouxED1v8_xQ1_iyX8RXKeC3CVT4dkgcGLfdbxN3kI=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba5\";s:6:\"rating\";i:5;s:4:\"text\";s:1133:\"This museum deserves a big round of applause ? and congratulatory remarks to the former contributors and working teams , to the current staff members and stakeholders.\nThe building boasts of the very popular and majestic TREE OF LIFE that has become an iconic feature of the museum.\nFurthermore, the longest or biggest crocodile  ever caught in the Philippines ? locally known as LOLONG had his skin and skeletal system preserved and now one of the special features of the museum.\nThe manner of curating the fauna, trees, animals, insects and many others is indeed deserving of compliments.\nThe museum is open daily… and there is no entrance fee . You may give a donation and drop it in the donation box by the main entrance.\nAccessibility is of paramount importance here. The stairs are good and well maintained, the elevator ? also takes you to different levels while the ramps that are well-designed are a good option for us who love to walk .\nI did not take a lot of photos but I hope every Filipino should make time to visit this and appreciate this museum. Clearly, it opens our eyes to the richness of our heritage.\";s:9:\"timestamp\";s:30:\"2026-06-06T14:37:58.601389365Z\";s:6:\"source\";s:6:\"Google\";}i:2;a:6:{s:6:\"author\";s:11:\"Sobi Thomas\";s:6:\"avatar\";s:123:\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/ALV-UjVaNr7XFddvPvifGX8R1xAQKjL7GQO_BjJObUxSjW8veoQkCzIs=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba5\";s:6:\"rating\";i:5;s:4:\"text\";s:883:\"Visited  and had a wonderful experience. The museum is beautifully designed and offers a fascinating journey through the natural history and biodiversity of the Philippines. The exhibits are well organized, informative, and engaging for visitors of all ages.\n\nThe impressive displays of plants, animals, ecosystems, and geological formations provide a deeper understanding of the country\'s rich natural heritage. The architecture of the museum itself is remarkable, with its stunning central atrium creating a memorable visual experience.\n\nThe museum combines education and inspiration, making it an excellent destination for students, families, researchers, and tourists. It is one of the best places in Manila to learn about nature, conservation, and the incredible biodiversity of the Philippines.\n\nHighly recommended for anyone interested in science, nature, and the environment.\";s:9:\"timestamp\";s:30:\"2026-06-04T06:23:26.849100691Z\";s:6:\"source\";s:6:\"Google\";}i:3;a:6:{s:6:\"author\";s:15:\"Dr. Ajeet Singh\";s:6:\"avatar\";s:125:\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/ALV-UjUpvZJd9QbCCs3RIPR07wHOV8l34Wrku5kFcV4FnHvSLw5wsljkKA=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba5\";s:6:\"rating\";i:4;s:4:\"text\";s:1792:\"As an Indian tourist visiting Manila, the National Museum of Natural History was an excellent choice for a half-day afternoon visit, especially after exploring the historic district of Intramuros. The museum is conveniently located and provides a refreshing change from historical sightseeing.\n\nOne of the best things about this museum is that entry is completely free, making it a great attraction for both local and international visitors. The queue was well managed and quite reasonable during my visit. Visitors are required to deposit their bags at the baggage counter before entering, which helps maintain security and protects the exhibits.\n\nA useful tip for first-time visitors: start by going to the basement level and then take the elevator directly to the top floor. From there, you can gradually walk down through each level while exploring the exhibits. This route makes the visit much more organized and enjoyable.\n\nThe museum houses a rich collection showcasing the incredible biodiversity, geology, wildlife, and natural heritage of the Philippines. The exhibits are informative, well presented, and provide valuable insight into the country\'s unique ecosystems and natural treasures.\n\nThe building itself is impressive, featuring beautiful architecture and a stunning central atrium that becomes one of the highlights of the visit. The museum is clean, well maintained, and equipped with toilet facilities, making it comfortable for families and tourists.\n\nOverall, the National Museum of Natural History is a must-visit attraction for any foreign tourist visiting Manila. If you have already visited Intramuros in the morning, this museum makes for a perfect afternoon destination, offering an educational, relaxing, and enriching experience—all completely free of charge.\";s:9:\"timestamp\";s:30:\"2026-05-31T10:08:42.074624994Z\";s:6:\"source\";s:6:\"Google\";}i:4;a:6:{s:6:\"author\";s:3:\"Ria\";s:6:\"avatar\";s:122:\"https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/a-/ALV-UjVkdC_2HhyK18fvzMAAwj4hUAJzbY5b7zh4sgM7R7YA7fI36eI=s128-c0x00000000-cc-rp-mo-ba6\";s:6:\"rating\";i:5;s:4:\"text\";s:419:\"Absolutely loved every corner of the National Museum of Natural History. For anyone who enjoys biology like I do, this place feels like a paradise. The way the information is presented makes it easy and enjoyable to learn.\n\nThere are plenty of natural history collections and clear illustrations throughout the museum, making the experience both educational and engaging. Definitely one of my favorite museums to visit.\";s:9:\"timestamp\";s:30:\"2026-01-18T12:34:23.249084626Z\";s:6:\"source\";s:6:\"Google\";}}s:17:\"editorial_summary\";s:97:\"Zoological, botanical & geological specimens displayed in an elegant venue with a soaring atrium.\";s:6:\"source\";s:6:\"google\";}i:17;a:15:{s:2:\"id\";s:27:\"ChIJY1NEIz3KlzMRamMm7IEXLWw\";s:4:\"name\";s:88:\"Archdiocesan Shrine of Nuestra Señora de la Consolación y Correa - San Agustín Church\";s:3:\"lat\";d:14.589077399999999;s:3:\"lng\";d:120.97535099999999;s:7:\"address\";s:67:\"General Luna St, Intramuros, Manila, 1002 Metro Manila, Philippines\";s:8:\"category\